WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.380
SPEAKER 1: to Get
Cooking in Cloud,

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:06.450
where we shared the best recipes
to apply in your cloud kitchen,

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:07.800
I'm Priyanka Vergadia.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.770
And in this episode, we will
talk about scaling a web

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.320
application on Google
Compute Engine.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:26.390
As we learned in our
Compute Engine example,

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.160
when web apps are
deployed on GCE instances,

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.190
deployment and scaling
is done automatically

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:36.490
and seamlessly by using
instants templates.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.930
When your website
becomes popular,

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.170
and the users integral
from 1 to 1 million,

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.080
you need to add instances.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.600
And when the requests
go back down,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.700
you need to remove
those instances

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.440
to keep the costs low.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:55.380
But how does adding and removing
instances automatically work?

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.300
Compute Engine
autoscaling policies.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.510
Autoscaling is one of the
features of managed instance

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:02.440
groups.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.130
Managed instance groups allow
you to operate applications

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.250
on multiple identical
virtual machines based

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:10.930
on instance templates.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:14.560
You can make your workload
scalable and highly available

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.750
by taking advantage of
automated managed instance group

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.910
services, including
autoscaling, autohealing,

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:25.850
regional deployments,
and auto-updating.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:26.590
But wait.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:28.390
What is an instance template?

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:32.500
An instance template is
a specific, customized

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.410
configuration of
the GCE instance

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:39.310
that facilitates the reuse
of instance configurations

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.740
by using managed
instance groups.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.980
This custom image then
becomes the starting point

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.580
for future deployments.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.780
Now, back to
autoscaling policies.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.160
Autoscaling policies provides
a way to add or remove

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.690
instances as needed.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:00.400
The impact of autoscaling
policies is twofold.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.820
One, your users get
a great experience

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.580
using your application
because there are always

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:09.310
enough resources
to meet the demand.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:10.240
And two.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.030
You maintain better
control over your costs

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:17.380
because the autoscaler removes
the instances when demand falls

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.200
below a specific threshold.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.410
To create an
autoscaler, you must

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:27.250
specify the autoscaling policy
and a target utilization level

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.800
that the autoscaler
uses to determine

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.630
when to scale the group.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:37.000
You can choose to scale using
average CPU utilization,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.740
Stackdriver monitoring
metrics, or HTTP load balancing

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:45.220
serving capacity, which can
be based on either utilization

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.580
or requests per second.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.420
The autoscaler continuously
collects usage information

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.560
based on the policy, compares
the actual utilization

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.350
to your desired
target utilization,

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:03.580
and determines if the group
needs to be scaled up or down.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:07.890
For example, if you scale
based on CPU utilization,

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:11.940
you can set your target
utilization level at 80%,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:16.290
and the autoscaler will
pull for the CPU utilization

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.350
and check if it
is more than 80%.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:23.490
If so, it adds the new
instance to the instance group.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.830
If the CPU utilization
is less than 80%,

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:29.640
then it removes an
instance from the group,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.510
making sure the capacity
is always maintained.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:37.350
You can use autoscaling in
conjunction with load balancing

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.410
by setting up an autoscaler
that scales based

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.420
on the load of your instances.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.940
For example, assume
the load balancing

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.270
serving capacity of a
managed instance group

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:53.080
is defined as 100 requests
per second, for instance.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.330
If you create an autoscaler
with HTTP load balancing policy

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:02.610
and set it to maintain a target
utilization level of 80%,

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:06.300
the autoscaler will
add or remove instances

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.000
from a managed instance
group to maintain

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.590
80% of the serving capacity,
or 80 requests per second

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:14.950
per instance.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:19.170
Now, our architecture needs
to automatically replace

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:24.030
instances that have failed
or have become unavailable.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.640
And when the new
instance comes online,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.520
it should understand
its role in the system.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.310
It should configure
itself automatically.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.380
And it should discover
any of the dependencies.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:38.880
And it should start handling
requests automatically.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.340
To replace a field
instance automatically,

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:45.750
we can use several Compute
Engine components together.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.550
You could create
instance templates

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:50.700
that use a public image
and a startup script

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.240
to prepare the instance
after it starts running.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.480
But we recommend that you
use deterministic instance

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:01.620
templates, which minimizes
risks and unexpected behavior

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.480
from your instance templates.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.250
Thanks to Managed
Instance Groups,

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:09.120
now we have a system that can
replace unhealthy instances

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.410
with new ones.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.110
But we still have a challenge.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:17.020
How are we going to know
which instance to replace?

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:22.080
Well for that, we need to define
what is an unhealthy instance.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.590
And to do that, we
use health checks.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:28.770
We recommend that you use
separate health checks for load

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:32.220
balancing and for autohealing
Autohealing health

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:35.520
checks are set up at the
managed instance group level.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:40.050
You create a health check that
looks for a response on port 80

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.350
and that can tolerate
some failure before it

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:45.900
marks instance as
unhealthy and causes

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.700
them to be [INAUDIBLE] created.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:51.570
In this example, an instance
is marked as healthy

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:54.720
if it returns
successfully two times.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.550
It is marked as unhealthy if
it returns unsuccessful three

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.560
consecutive times.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.230
We learned some tricks to
make your web application

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.750
resilient by spinning
up new instances

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:10.110
and taking them down if
compute resources fail

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:11.820
or your traffic grows.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.610
That's all for today on
Get Cooking in Cloud.

